Notes for the writer, not part of the body: the body starts at the `**Status:**` line below the rule, and
nothing above the rule is written to Leeway.

Two owners, one body: the product owner writes everything down to "Open questions for dev" with `shape-project`, and
`plan-project` checks it without editing it; after that check passes, the planner adds "Technical criteria" with
`plan-project`. Neither touches the other's part; the Status line is shared, and each sets its own half. A body
written by hand in this shape counts the same.

- Replace every `<placeholder>`. A heading with nothing to say reads "n/a: <why>". Delete an optional section
  rather than leave it empty, except Design, where "Design link missing" is written out.
- An answer nobody has decided yet stays under its heading as one line starting "OPEN:", never a guess; the
  OPEN: lines are what is still open, and the Status line does not count them.
- Numbers are stable, because tasks cite them: a new criterion goes at the end, and a dropped one stays as
  "AC-n (removed: <why>)". Never renumber.
- Short, and by reference: what can be looked up in the code or a doc is a pointer to where, not a retelling.
- Leeway shows raw HTML as text, so the body carries no HTML comment or other marker.
- A description holds at most 5,000 characters. If the body would not fit, `plan-project` moves the whole
  Technical criteria section to one story comment whose body opens with the heading `# Technical criteria`
  (the latest one wins), and leaves a pointer here.

